{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.nilpotent-ideal.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.003cb1cc70cf5a2c.nilpotent-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"An ideal","editorial_reason":"The Stacks 10.32.1 nilpotence condition is imposed on an ideal in a commutative ring; a two-sided ideal supplies the underlying absorption and additive-subgroup structure, not the nilpotence exponent.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AMF","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.locally-nilpotent-ideal.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.003cb1cc70cf5a2c.locally-nilpotent-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"An ideal","editorial_reason":"Stacks 10.32.1 quantifies over elements of a ring ideal; the independently reconstructed two-sided ideal supplies that underlying structure, without conflating elementwise nilpotence with ideal-power nilpotence.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AMF","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.quasicompact-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.27eb121021fd989d.quasicompact-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"The Stacks 5.12.1 finite-subcover property presupposes an arbitrary topological space and its open subsets; no Hausdorff requirement is introduced by the prerequisite link.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.noetherian-topological-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.2d96a50b600ecbfc.noetherian-topological-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"The descending-chain condition on closed sets is a property of a topological space, not ring Noetherianity; the linked open-set axioms supply the correct underlying topological context.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0050","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.topology-basis.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.6a3376e5ecb82bfb.topology-basis","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"The Stacks definition of a basis quantifies over the open subsets and points of a topological space, so a general topological-space meaning is the appropriate foundational prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.specialization-topological.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.672a4a502273760f.specialization-topological","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Specialization is defined using closure of a singleton in an arbitrary topological space. The new target supplies the ambient topology; a separate closure-of-subset meaning remains to be sourced.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0060","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.dedekind-domain.to.commutative-prime-ideal","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.dedekind-domain","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-prime-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"prime ideals","editorial_reason":"Stacks 10.120.14 factors nonzero ideals into prime ideals of an integral domain; the commutative-unital proper-prime-ideal target is a matching prerequisite, not the notion of prime elements.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3APrime_Ideal_of_Ring/Commutative_and_Unitary_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.dedekind-domain.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.dedekind-domain","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ideal","editorial_reason":"Ideal factorization in a Dedekind domain is factorization of ring ideals in a commutative ring, for which the two-sided ideal notion is a sound structural prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.principal-ideal-domain.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.05531f966e3a206f.principal-ideal-domain","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ideal","editorial_reason":"The Stacks 10.120.12 principal-ideal-domain property applies to every ideal of an integral domain. A commutative ring ideal is a two-sided ideal; generation by one element is an additional condition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034O","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.chain-of-prime-ideals.to.commutative-prime-ideal","from_formulation":"form.22c049b8eee93897.chain-of-prime-ideals","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-prime-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"prime ideals","editorial_reason":"A chain in Stacks 10.60.1 is a sequence of prime ideals ordered by strict inclusion in a commutative unital ring. The target supplies exactly the proper prime-ideal product criterion.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3APrime_Ideal_of_Ring/Commutative_and_Unitary_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.jacobson-ring-prime-characterization.to.commutative-prime-ideal","from_formulation":"form.03b55b8ca41abb20.jacobson-ring-prime-characterization","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.commutative-prime-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"prime ideal","editorial_reason":"The equivalent Jacobson-ring characterization involves intersections of maximal ideals containing each prime ideal. The new commutative prime-ideal meaning is a valid partial prerequisite; maximal ideals remain unresolved.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FZ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3APrime_Ideal_of_Ring/Commutative_and_Unitary_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch02.homogeneous-ideal.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.29b2c34cc33d4b62.homogeneous-ideal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ideal I","editorial_reason":"The Stacks graded-ring formulation first requires an ordinary ring ideal and then imposes graded-submodule conditions. Two-sided ideal is the underlying prerequisite; no nonnegative grading restriction is inferred.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JM","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_source_check_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
